  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nRelatively quiet weather conditions with seasonably cool \ntemperatures are expected for the next couple days as mid-level \nridging edges northeastward across the Great Lakes region. \nUncharacteristic of early August, low stratus with embedded \npatches of drizzle continue to filter over northeast Illinois \nthis afternoon in response to abundant moisture trapped under \nthe incoming subsidence inversion. Given a persistent corridor \nof upstream low-level clouds over central Lake Michigan, \nsaturated ground conditions from Saturday's heavy rain, and \nclearing skies aloft, have some concern that low-level stratus \n(possibly lowering to the ground as fog) will expand \nsouthwestward under the inversion overnight. Have accordingly \nincreased cloud cover and added patchy fog for a large portion \nof the CWA during this time. Otherwise, dangerous swimming \nconditions will continue into Monday morning before winds \ngradually diminish.\n\nLate Tuesday night through Thursday, a large upper-level low \nover southern Hudson Bay will push a slowing/stalling cold front\nnear or over the CWA. A seasonably strong kinematic environment\nhighlighted by a 100+ knot upper-level jet across the far Upper\nMississippi River Valley has the making for a drawn out precip \nevent with a mix of warm season convection and cool season \nanafrontal elements. Adding in a well-timed passing mid-level \nshortwave interacting with a very moist airmass (PWATs nearing 2\ninches), will need to monitor for a non-zero severe storm risk,\nand especially renewed flooding potential Wednesday into early \nThursday. \n\nLow-level WAA and moisture advection will quickly return by \nearly next weekend while a broad low-amplitude ridge drifts \nacross the western CONUS. Expect summerlike warmth and \nincreasing humidity for the weekend. Building instability and \nmodest WNW flow aloft will also support increasing chances for \nconvective episodes into the Great Lakes region by Sunday.\n\nKluber",
